Well, I think we understand that perfectly well. This behaviour has
existed since at least Hack 1.0.3, and hence through every version of
NetHack released by the Dev Team. It is clearly well-understood, with no
unforeseen consequences, not overly difficult to alter (probably a
one-liner, just another check along with the difficulty of the target
monster relative to the pet), and has been like this for well over a
decade. The program is clearly acting as intended.

In fact, in dogmove.c, there's already a check for 

mtmp2->peaceful && !Conflict 

it's just at the moment it's &&ed with a check if the pet's HP are low, or
the potential target is a Quest friendly. So it's a matter of removing

                         (mtmp->mhp*4 < mtmp->mhpmax
                          || mtmp2->data->msound == MS_GUARDIAN
                          || mtmp2->data->msound == MS_LEADER) &&

[careful, only one bracket from the first line]
which I suspect is not beyond the collective abilities of the Dev Team.
